#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100566 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a32f8ec3-ac69-5b6a-9937-1093556d24d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Trigonometric Equation Containing Sine or Cosine"}] :question/id #uuid "b52808fa-1f08-53ac-a5e0-97dd05b1e57b" :question/math-academy-id "e-3552" :question/problem "Find the general solution to $2\\cos{x} + \\sqrt{2}= 0$, where $x$ is measured in degrees." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\cos x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\cos x + \\sqrt{2} &= 0 \\\\\n2\\cos x &= -\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n\\cos x &= -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx_1 = \\arccos\\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right)= 135^\\circ\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 45^\\circ\n$$\n\nSince $\\cos x< 0$, the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 45^\\circ\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1258/Source/Images/8584042d6ef9bb2998a7c62ae2b7e609.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= 180^{∘} + θ_{R} \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} + 45^{∘} \\\\\n&= 225^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions for $[MATH: x\\in[0,360)]$ are\n\n$$\nx=135^\\circ, 225^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution for the given equation, where $n$ is an integer, is\n\n$$\nx = 135^\\circ+n\\cdot 360^\\circ, \\quad x=225^\\circ+n\\cdot 360^\\circ\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100567 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa20dbfa-3f6f-5caf-9c10-906f2422f161" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Trigonometric Equation Containing Tangent or Cotangent"}] :question/id #uuid "6831fce9-9c10-5c75-9431-529b852c9bed" :question/math-academy-id "e-3553" :question/problem "What is the general solution to the equation $\\cot{x}=1$?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\cot{x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\tan{x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan x &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx_1 = \\arctan \\left(1 \\right)= \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of $\\pi$. Therefore, the general solution for the given equation is:\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}+n\\pi\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100568 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a231e4f-d6b3-549f-81d4-30c0a883f9b4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Trigonometric Equation Containing Secant or Cosecant"}] :question/id #uuid "5a599246-2feb-59fa-b859-3b9bd20c9e57" :question/math-academy-id "e-3554" :question/problem "What is the general solution to the equation $3\\csc x+2\\sqrt{3}=0$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\csc x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3\\csc x + 2\\sqrt{3} &= 0 \\\\\n3\\csc x &= -2\\sqrt{3} \\\\\n\\csc x &= -\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\csc{x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} =-\\dfrac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}}=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx_{1} = \\arcsin (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) = \\frac{5π}{3}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $4$th quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nSince $\\sin x< 0$, the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1258/Source/Images/55452604d3a171e9228cc769aae317ea.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= π + θ_{R} \\\\\n&= π + \\frac{π}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions for $[MATH: x\\in[0,2\\pi)]$ are\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}+2n\\pi, \\quad x=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}+2n\\pi\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100569 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff04ceeb-c9f6-5562-b494-204eb62d7f5b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of the Points of Intersection of a Trigonometric Curve and a Horizontal Line"}] :question/id #uuid "4457a33e-7022-53b2-bcad-f565ec762f2c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3555" :question/problem "The graph shows a portion of the curve $y=\\sec{x}$ and the line $y=\\sqrt{2}$. What is the general solution for the $x$-values of all of their points of intersection?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1258/Source/Images/42b069cebd215708e6a018e0e3ecc90d.png)" :question/worked-solution "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\sec x =\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\sec{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos{x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{\\cos x} &= \\sqrt{2} \\\\\n\\cos x &= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx_1 = \\arccos \\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right)= 45^\\circ\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 45^\\circ\n$$\n\nSince $\\cos x > 0$, the second solution must lie in the $4$th quadrant, and it also has\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 45^\\circ\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1258/Source/Images/a8b3e42e6690c60434773d04d3b5d73d.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= 360^{∘} - θ_{R} \\\\\n&= 360^{∘} - 45^{∘} \\\\\n&= 315^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions for $[MATH: x\\in\\left[0^\\circ,360^\\circ\\right)]$ are\n\n$$\nx=45^\\circ, 315^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx = 45^\\circ+n\\cdot 360^\\circ, \\quad x=315^\\circ+n\\cdot 360^\\circ\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101147 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa20dbfa-3f6f-5caf-9c10-906f2422f161" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Trigonometric Equation Containing Tangent or Cotangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad76bd79-29ec-5162-b3b5-6767fbbd167f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 60^{∘} + n \\cdot 180^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098653} {:answer/id #uuid "ef2c5383-78cf-502a-b9b3-647a6546ef75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 90^{∘} + n \\cdot 180^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098654} {:answer/id #uuid "186d6335-deb2-5be6-ab27-0d972f0f25ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 30^{∘} + n \\cdot 180^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098655} {:answer/id #uuid "28f9c780-09a0-5072-9d82-dcd078517ab1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-30^{∘} + n \\cdot 180^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098656} {:answer/id #uuid "7e8719c2-5250-5d34-89aa-de9912ce2f7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-60^{∘} + n \\cdot 180^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e8719c2-5250-5d34-89aa-de9912ce2f7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-60^{∘} + n \\cdot 180^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098657} :answer-field/id #uuid "e75cd6ad-07e3-55a6-8811-6d153734ac99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098652}] :question/id #uuid "c8aaf6f0-933e-536c-8ff9-c54f73295028" :question/math-academy-id "q-16229" :question/problem "In degrees, find the general solution to the equation $\\tan x =-\\sqrt{3}$. Assume that $n$ is an integer."}]}
